---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/how-to-get-2fa-code/" jsonLd={jsonLd} alternates={alternates}>
  <SiteHeader />
  <main id="main">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-[820px] px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 lg:py-24 xl:px-0">
      <p class="section-label">2FA How-to</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl tracking-normal text-ink sm:text-5xl">
        How to get your 2FA code
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-lg font-medium text-ink">
        The whole process takes about a minute: reveal the setup key, paste it here, and type the
        resulting code into whatever screen asked for it. No phone required — the reference
        implementation of the math is running at the top of our{" "}
        <a href="/">home page generator</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="rich-text mt-10">
        <h2>What you need before you start</h2>
        <p>
          Account access, obviously — you should already be signed in to the service you want to
          protect. Beyond that, two things matter. First, the service must offer an
          <strong>authenticator app</strong> option as its second factor; that covers Google,
          Microsoft, GitHub, Discord, Steam, AWS, Coinbase, Kraken, Binance, X, and most banks and
          exchanges. Second, you need somewhere to <em>run</em> the authenticator: a phone app,
          or — the point of this site — this browser tool, which computes the identical codes
          without installing anything.
        </p>
        <p>
          A note on naming: services call this second factor “two-factor authentication”, “2FA”,
          “two-step verification”, “TOTP”, or “authenticator app”. Behind the labels it is the
          same thing — a time-based, one-time code derived from a shared secret. If you want the
          mechanism explained rather than used, read{" "}
          <a href="/what-is-2fa/">What is 2FA? How TOTP works</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 1: Turn on 2FA and reveal the setup key</h2>
        <p>
          Open your account’s security settings and find the section for two-factor
          authentication. Choose the <em>authenticator app</em> option. The service displays a QR
          code for you to scan with a phone — and that screen almost always carries an
          alternative: a link reading <em>“can’t scan?”</em>, <em>“enter code manually”</em>, or
          <em>“setup key”</em>. Following that link reveals the actual secret: a Base32 string
          like <code>JBSWY3DPEHPK3PXP</code>, occasionally wrapped in a full{" "}
          <code>otpauth://</code> URI that also carries the account label, digit count, refresh
          period, and hash algorithm.
        </p>
        <p>
          This key <strong>is</strong> the credential — it is the movable equivalent of your
          password for the second factor. Treat the screen like a recovery screen: nobody
          legitimate will ever ask you to paste a setup key into an email or a chat. Copy it,
          finish enabling 2FA (most services ask for one working code to confirm), and keep the
          original somewhere encrypted, like a password manager.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 2: Paste the key into the generator</h2>
        <p>
          In the <a href="/">generator at the top of our home page</a>, paste what you copied —
          the bare Base32 string or the full <code>otpauth://</code> link, both are fine. A live
          6-digit code appears within seconds and refreshes every 30 seconds. You can also import
          the key from an image: paste a screenshot of the QR code directly onto the page with
          Ctrl+V, drop an image file onto it, or scan the QR code with your camera — decode
          happens locally, and nothing is uploaded. If the service hands out a nonstandard
          configuration (8-digit codes, 15/30/60-second periods, SHA-256, or counter-based
          HOTP), a full <code>otpauth://</code> URI carries all of those parameters and the
          generator honors them automatically.
        </p>

        <h2>Step 3: Copy the live code and finish sign-in</h2>
        <p>
          Type the current code into the verification prompt before it rotates. Each code is
          valid for one 30-second window (shorter or longer for services with custom periods), so
          if the badge hits the last few seconds, wait for the next code rather than rushing a
          stale one. From now on, every sign-in with this service repeats the same loop: open
          the generator → copy the code → paste it in. Your last key is remembered{" "}
          <a href="/privacy/">in your own browser’s local storage only</a>, so the next visit is
          one click away.
        </p>

        <h2>Where to find the setup key on popular services</h2>
        <p>
          Each service buries the authenticator option in a slightly different spot, so we
          publish step-by-step <a href="/guides/">setup guides</a> that pair with this
          generator — ending with you pasting the key here and finishing the setup. The most
          requested: <a href="/guides/google/">Google</a>,{" "}
          <a href="/guides/microsoft/">Microsoft</a>, <a href="/guides/discord/">Discord</a>,{" "}
          <a href="/guides/github/">GitHub</a>, <a href="/guides/steam/">Steam</a>,{" "}
          <a href="/guides/aws/">AWS</a>, <a href="/guides/coinbase/">Coinbase</a>,{" "}
          <a href="/guides/kraken/">Kraken</a>, and <a href="/guides/binance/">Binance</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Troubleshooting: what to do when it doesn’t work</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>The code is rejected.</strong> In practice this is nearly always clock drift on
          the device running the generator. TOTP compares your code against a code derived from
          the <em>service’s</em> clock; if your system time is off by even half a minute, the two
          diverge. Set your operating system to sync time automatically (Windows: “Sync now” in
          date &amp; time settings; macOS: “Set date and time automatically”), then try the next
          code.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The QR code won’t scan.</strong> Use the <em>“can’t scan?”</em> link to reveal
          the setup key instead — the text key is always more reliable than photographing a
          screen. If you only have a screenshot, crop it to the QR code itself; heavily
          compressed or angled shots often fail to decode.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You lost the phone that had the key.</strong> That is the exact situation this
          tool was built for: if you kept the original secret in your password manager — or if
          you pasted it here while the phone still worked — you can regenerate codes on any
          computer. No phone, no restore, no support ticket.
        </p>

        <h2>Two habits worth forming</h2>
        <p>
          Save the service’s <strong>recovery codes</strong> offline when you enable 2FA; they
          are the bail-out if you ever lose the device holding the secret. And never treat a
          live 2FA code as something to share: no support agent, no bank, no exchange ever needs
          your current code unsolicited. A code is only ever useful within its 30-second window
          at the login screen you chose.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Layout>
